rnal capsule is the most plausible site of perfusion impairment, as it is a common location for ischemic strokes that cause pure sensory deficits. Immediate medical intervention is necessary to prevent further neurological damage and to manage the underlying cerebrovascular risk factors, such as hypertension.''' \n", "vote_history": [{"Neurology": "yes", "Emergency Medicine": "yes", "Vascular Medicine": "yes", "Internal Medicine": "yes", "Cardiology": "yes", "Neuroanatomy": "yes"}], "revision_history": [], "syn_repo_history": ["Question: A 58-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of sudden-onset right-sided body numbness for 1 hour. He reports that he has a 15-year history of hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide. He is alert and oriented to time, place, and person. Neurological examination shows decreased sensation to light pinprick and temperature on the right side of the face and body. Motor strength is 5/5 and deep tendon reflexes are 2+ bilaterally. Perfusion of which of the following structures of the brain is most likely impaired in this patient? \nOptions: {'A': 'Parietal lobe', 'B': 'Posterior limb of the internal capsule', 'C': 'Lateral medulla', 'D': 'Basal pons', 'E': 'Ventral thalamus'} \nTotal Analysis: The patient's presentation of sudden-onset right-sided body numbness, along with preserved motor strength and reflexes, strongly indicates a cerebrovascular event, most likely an ischemic stroke or TIA. The patient's history of hypertension increases the risk of such an event. The neurological examination findings, particularly the decreased sensation on the right side of the body, suggest a lesion affecting the left hemisphere, specifically the left primary sensory cortex and the left thalamus. Among the provided options, the posterior limb of the internal capsule is the most plausible site of perfusion impairment, as it is a common location for ischemic strokes that cause pure sensory deficits. Immediate medical intervention is necessary to prevent further neurological damage and to manage the underlying cerebrovascular risk factors, such as hypertension.''' \n"], "raw_output": "Option: B", "error": null}
{"question": "A 31-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 4-week history of a painless lump near the left wrist and tingling pain over his left hand. Physical examination shows a transilluminating, rubbery, fixed, non-tender mass over the lateral volar aspect of the left wrist. There is decreased sensation to pinprick on the thumb, index finger, middle finger, and radial half of the ring finger of the left hand. The tingling pain is aggravated by tapping over the swelling. Which of the following adjacent structures is at risk of entrapment if this mass persists?", "options": {"A": "Ulnar artery", "B": "Extensor pollicis brevis tendon", "C": "Flexor pollicis longus tendon", "D": "Flexor carpi radialis tendon", "E": "Ulnar nerve"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Orthopedics", "Neurology", "Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation", "Radiology", "Surgery"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Orthopedics"], "question_analyses": {"Orthopedics": "The scenario described suggests a condition known as a ganglion cyst, which is a common benign swelling that can occur near joints or tendons. In this case, the patient presents with a painless lump near the left wrist and tingling pain over the left hand. The physical examination findings of a transilluminating, rubbery, fixed, non-tender mass over the lateral volar aspect of the left wrist, along with decreased sensation to pinprick on the thumb, index finger, middle finger, and radial half of the ring finger, are indicative of a ganglion cyst. The tingling pain being aggravated by tapping over the swelling (Tinel's sign) further supports this diagnosis. The most alarming aspect of this condition is the involvement of the median nerve. The symptoms of decreased sensation in the thumb, index finger, middle finger, and radial half of the ring finger suggest that the median nerve is being compressed. This compression is likely due to the ganglion cyst located near the carpal tunnel, which is a na